findings.detail:
From the Porto/ithacaxyz account source that Altana pins:

  if (tokenPeriodSpend.lastUpdated < current) {
      tokenPeriodSpend.lastUpdated = current;
      tokenPeriodSpend.spent = 0;
  }
  if ((tokenPeriodSpend.spent += amount) > tokenPeriodSpend.limit) {
      revert ExceededSpendLimit(token);
  }

`current` comes from startOfSpendPeriod(...), the same pure function I called live on chain 56 above. setSpendLimit is `onlyThis` + checkKeyHashIsNonZero, i.e. only settable by the account acting on itself (an admin-signed intent), not by the session key it constrains.

Caveat on provenance: the exact revert-error selector keccak("ExceededSpendLimit(address)")=0x9054c912 did NOT appear as a literal in the deployed runtime, and neither did Unauthorized(). Solady-style assembly reverts and optimizer packing can hide selector literals from a naive hex grep, and the function-selector evidence plus the live startOfSpendPeriod/spendInfos calls are independently conclusive. I am reporting the exact revert wording as source-derived (from ithacaxyz/account main), not as byte-matched against v0.5.10.

grantSession passes `permissions` into keyDescriptorFromSigner and hands the descriptor to submitCalls, which calls Porto's prepareCalls against Altana's relay, then signs whatever digests come back:

  const prepared = await prepareCalls(client, prepareParams);
  for (const [name, digest] of Object.entries(prepared.digests ?? {})) {
      signatures[name] = await signer.signDigest(digest);
  }

There is no client-side re-derivation or assertion that the returned intent actually contains setSpendLimit with the requested limit/period/token. A buggy or hostile relay could return an intent that authorizes the session key WITHOUT the spend limit, and the admin would sign it.

This does not make Altana T1. Once the limit IS on-chain, a compromised session key cannot exceed it by any route — the account contract reverts regardless of the relay. But the honest claim is narrower than "survives even a compromised AiKi": it survives a compromised agent/session key and a compromised relay at execution time; the provisioning step trusts Altana's relay unless the integrator verifies spendInfos(keyHash) after the grant.

From the SDK's own type declarations (dist/internal/sessions.d.ts), with the doc comment "A spending cap for a token over a rolling period":

  type SpendPermission = { limit: bigint; period: "minute"|"hour"|"day"|"week"|"month"|"year"; token?: Address }
  type CallPermission  = { signature: string; to: Address } | { signature: string } | { to: Address }
  type SessionPermissions = { calls?: readonly CallPermission[]; spend?: readonly SpendPermission[] }

Compilation targets on chain 56:
  spend.limit + spend.period + spend.token -> setSpendLimit(bytes32 keyHash, address token, uint8 period, uint256 limit), read back via spendInfos(bytes32), enforced in _incrementSpent
  calls.to / calls.signature -> setCanExecute(bytes32,address,bytes4,bool), checked via canExecute(bytes32,address,bytes), reverts UnauthorizedCall(bytes32,address,bytes) (selector 0xf78c1b53, present in the deployed runtime)
  expiry -> the uint40 expiry field on the Key struct returned by getKeys() (selector 0x2150c518, present)

`token` omitted means the native asset (0x0…0). The SDK header comment states it directly: "enforced on-chain by the Altana account contract: calls outside the granted permissions revert at validation time."

interfaces.body:
/** A spending cap for a token over a rolling period. */
export type SpendPermission = {
    limit: bigint;
    period: "minute" | "hour" | "day" | "week" | "month" | "year";
    /** Omit for native token (ETH). */
    token?: Address;
};

/** A single allowed call rule. AND semantics between the optional fields. */
export type CallPermission =
    | { signature: string; to: Address }
    | { signature: string }
    | { to: Address };

export type SessionPermissions = {
    /** Allowed calls. If omitted, all targets are allowed (use carefully). */
    calls?: readonly CallPermission[];
    /** Per-token spending caps. */
    spend?: readonly SpendPermission[];
};

// selector -> confirmed present in deployed runtime on BNB Smart Chain (56)
function setSpendLimit(bytes32 keyHash, address token, SpendPeriod period, uint256 limit); // 0x598daac4
function removeSpendLimit(bytes32 keyHash, address token, SpendPeriod period);             // 0x2081a278
function spendInfos(bytes32 keyHash) view returns (SpendInfo[]);                           // 0xdcc09ebf  (live eth_call OK)
function startOfSpendPeriod(uint256 unixTimestamp, SpendPeriod period) pure returns (uint256); // 0xfaba56d8 (live eth_call OK)
function setCanExecute(bytes32 keyHash, address target, bytes4 fnSel, bool can);           // 0x136a12f7
function canExecute(bytes32 keyHash, address target, bytes data) view returns (bool);      // 0xff619c6b
function getKeys() view returns (Key[] keys, bytes32[] keyHashes);                          // 0x2150c518
error UnauthorizedCall(bytes32 keyHash, address target, bytes data);                        // 0xf78c1b53

enum SpendPeriod { Minute, Hour, Day, Week, Month, Year, Forever }

// NONE of the above selectors appear in KeyStore 0x6572427E... or KeyStoreController 0x0834Ee2C...

// rolling window: entering a new period zeroes the counter
if (tokenPeriodSpend.lastUpdated < current) {
    tokenPeriodSpend.lastUpdated = current;
    tokenPeriodSpend.spent = 0;
}
// the cap itself
if ((tokenPeriodSpend.spent += amount) > tokenPeriodSpend.limit) {
    revert ExceededSpendLimit(token);
}

// `current` = startOfSpendPeriod(block.timestamp, period)
// setSpendLimit is `onlyThis checkKeyHashIsNonZero(keyHash)` —
// only the account acting on itself (admin-signed intent) can set it;
// the constrained session key cannot raise its own cap.
